#sec("I", "Problem Formulation and Mathematical Model")

Time series generated by physical, physiological, or behavioral processes frequently experience nonlinear local time accelerations and decelerations: two recordings of the same underlying phenomenon may stretch or compress individual sub-events unevenly. Rigid lock-step metrics such as Euclidean distance and global translation metrics such as $"SBD"$ (Alg. 162) fail to capture such local elastic deformations. Dynamic Time Warping (DTW) @sakoe1978dtw resolves pairwise elastic comparison via dynamic programming, but embedding DTW inside centroid-based $k$-Means @lloyd1982least requires solving a nontrivial Fréchet mean problem: averaging a set of variable-length sequences under a non-Euclidean elastic discrepancy. Petitjean, Ketterlin, and Gançarski @petitjean2011dba introduced *DTW Barycenter Averaging (DBA)*, a global heuristic-free coordinate refinement algorithm that iteratively updates a sequence centroid by averaging all observations warped onto each centroid index.

Let $X = {x_1, dots, x_n}$ be a dataset of univariate sequences with $x_i = (x_(i, 1), dots, x_(i, L_i))^top in bb(R)^(L_i)$ (and $L_i = m$ in the equal-length regime). For a candidate barycenter $mu = (mu_1, dots, mu_m)^top in bb(R)^m$ and sequence $x in bb(R)^L$, define the pairwise squared-error cost matrix $Delta(mu, x) in bb(R)^(m times L)$ by $Delta(mu, x)_(p, q) = (mu_p - x_q)^2$.

#definition(title: "Admissible Warping Paths, DTW, and DTW Barycenter")[
  A binary matrix $A in {0, 1}^(m times L)$ belongs to the set of *admissible warping paths* $cal(A)_(m, L)$ @sakoe1978dtw if its support forms an ordered sequence $((p_1, q_1), dots, (p_R, q_R))$ satisfying:
  (i) *Boundary conditions*: $(p_1, q_1) = (1, 1)$ and $(p_R, q_R) = (m, L)$;
  (ii) *Monotonicity and continuity*: $(p_(r + 1) - p_r, q_(r + 1) - q_r) in {(1, 0), (0, 1), (1, 1)}$ for $1 <= r < R$.
  The *squared DTW discrepancy* is
  $ "DTW"^2 (mu, x) & = min_(A in cal(A)_(m, L)) chevron.l A, Delta(mu, x) chevron.r \
    & = min_(A in cal(A)_(m, L)) sum_(p, q : A_(p, q) = 1) (mu_p - x_q)^2, $
  and $"DTW"(mu, x) = sqrt("DTW"^2 (mu, x))$. For a cluster $C_k subset.eq {1, dots, n}$, the *DTW barycenter* minimizes the Fréchet inertia
  $ F_k (mu) = sum_(i in C_k) "DTW"^2 (mu, x_i), $
  and *DBA-based DTW $k$-Means* minimizes the global partition objective
  $ J_"DBA" (cal(C), {mu_k}) = sum_(k = 1)^K sum_(i in C_k) "DTW"^2 (mu_k, x_i). $
]

Given current iterate $mu^((r)) in bb(R)^m$, DBA computes an optimal warping matrix $A_i^((r)) in arg min_(A in cal(A)_(m, L_i)) chevron.l A, Delta(mu^((r)), x_i) chevron.r$ for every $i in C_k$ via Bellman recursion:
$ D_(p, q) & = (mu_p^((r)) - x_(i, q))^2 \
  & quad + min {D_(p - 1, q), D_(p, q - 1), D_(p - 1, q - 1)}, $
with $D_(0, 0) = 0$ and $D_(p, 0) = D_(0, q) = +infinity$ for $p, q >= 1$. Define the diagonal valence matrix $W_i^((r)) = "diag"(A_i^((r)) bold(1)_(L_i)) in bb(R)^(m times m)$, whose $p$-th diagonal entry counts how many elements of $x_i$ align with coordinate $mu_p^((r))$. Because boundary and step conditions force every admissible path to visit every row $p in {1, dots, m\}$ at least once, $W_i^((r)) succ.eq I_m$, so $sum_(i in C_k) W_i^((r)) succ.eq |C_k| I_m$ is strictly positive definite. The DBA update takes the exact closed form
$ mu^((r + 1)) = (sum_(i in C_k) W_i^((r)))^(-1) (sum_(i in C_k) A_i^((r)) x_i). $

#algbox(
  title: "DBA-Based DTW k-Means Clustering",
  inputs: [Series $X = {x_1, dots, x_n}$, cluster count $K$, outer cap $T$, inner DBA cap $I$],
  outputs: [Partition $cal(C) = {C_1, dots, C_K}$ and elastic barycenters ${mu_1, dots, mu_K}$],
)[
  1. *Initialize*: Select $K$ initial barycenters $mu_1^((0)), dots, mu_K^((0)) in bb(R)^m$ from $X$ (e.g., via medoid or DTW $k$-Means++ seeding).
  2. *Assignment*: For each $i in {1, dots, n}$, assign $z_i^((t)) = arg min_(1 <= k <= K) "DTW"^2 (mu_k^((t - 1)), x_i)$ using dynamic programming.
  3. *DBA Barycenter Refinement*: For each non-empty $C_k^((t))$, set $mu^((0)) = mu_k^((t - 1))$ and iterate for $r = 0, dots, I - 1$:
     (a) For each $i in C_k^((t))$, compute an optimal alignment path $A_i^((r)) in arg min_(A in cal(A)_(m, L_i)) chevron.l A, Delta(mu^((r)), x_i) chevron.r$ via DP traceback.
     (b) For each coordinate $p in {1, dots, m}$, compute valence $d_p^((r)) = sum_(i in C_k^((t))) (A_i^((r)) bold(1)_(L_i))_p$ and update
     $ mu_p^((r + 1)) = 1 / (d_p^((r))) sum_(i in C_k^((t))) (A_i^((r)) x_i)_p. $
     (c) Stop inner loop if $mu^((r + 1)) = mu^((r))$; set $mu_k^((t)) = mu^((r + 1))$.
  4. *Termination*: Repeat Steps 2--3 until $z^((t)) = z^((t - 1))$ or $t = T$.
]

#sec("II", "Formal Mathematical Proofs and Theoretical Guarantees")

We present five rigorous analytical results: exact Majorization-Minimization (MM) descent with quantitative step gain, a constructive finite counterexample proving non-convexity and strict suboptimality of DBA fixed points, affine equivariance and breakdown analysis, Delannoy path cardinality and DTW non-metricity, and band-constrained complexity guarantees.

#subsec("A", "Majorization-Minimization Descent and Finite Termination")

#theorem(title: "MM Lyapunov Descent and Finite Convergence of DBA", origin: "Original Unified Proof")[
  For any non-empty cluster $C_k$ and iterate $mu^((r)) in bb(R)^m$ with optimal paths ${A_i^((r))}_(i in C_k)$, define the quadratic majorizer @petitjean2011dba
  $ Q(mu | mu^((r))) & = sum_(i in C_k) chevron.l A_i^((r)), Delta(mu, x_i) chevron.r \
    & = sum_(i in C_k) sum_((p, q) in A_i^((r))) (mu_p - x_(i, q))^2. $
  Then: (i) $F_k (mu) <= Q(mu | mu^((r)))$ for all $mu in bb(R)^m$ with equality at $mu = mu^((r))$; (ii) the DBA update $mu^((r + 1))$ is the unique global minimizer of $Q(dot.op | mu^((r)))$; (iii) the Fréchet inertia obeys the strict quantitative Lyapunov descent
  $ F_k (mu^((r))) - F_k (mu^((r + 1))) & >= sum_(p = 1)^m d_p^((r)) (mu_p^((r)) - mu_p^((r + 1)))^2 \
    & >= |C_k| norm(mu^((r)) - mu^((r + 1)))_2^2; $
  (iv) both inner DBA and outer DTW $k$-Means terminate in finitely many iterations with monotonically non-increasing $J_"DBA"$.
]

#proof[
  *(i)* By definition of $"DTW"^2$, $F_k (mu) = sum_(i in C_k) min_(A_i in cal(A)_(m, L_i)) chevron.l A_i, Delta(mu, x_i) chevron.r <= sum_(i in C_k) chevron.l A_i^((r)), Delta(mu, x_i) chevron.r = Q(mu | mu^((r)))$ for all $mu$, with equality at $mu = mu^((r))$ because each $A_i^((r))$ is optimal at $mu^((r))$.

  *(ii)* Rewriting $Q(mu | mu^((r)))$ by centroid coordinate $p$ with association multiset $cal(S)_p^((r)) = {(i, q) : i in C_k, (A_i^((r)))_(p, q) = 1\}$ of cardinality $d_p^((r)) = |cal(S)_p^((r))|$ yields
  $ Q(mu | mu^((r))) = sum_(p = 1)^m sum_((i, q) in cal(S)_p^((r))) (mu_p - x_(i, q))^2. $
  Each path $A_i^((r)) in cal(A)_(m, L_i)$ starts at row $1$, ends at row $m$, and increments row indices by at most $1$ per step, so every row $p$ is visited at least once per series: $d_p^((r)) >= |C_k| >= 1$. Hence $Q(dot.op | mu^((r)))$ is separable and strongly convex with Hessian $2 "diag"(d_1^((r)), dots, d_m^((r))) succ.eq 2 |C_k| I_m$. Setting $partial Q / partial mu_p = 2 sum_((i, q) in cal(S)_p^((r))) (mu_p - x_(i, q)) = 0$ gives the unique global minimizer $mu_p^((r + 1)) = (d_p^((r)))^(-1) sum_((i, q) in cal(S)_p^((r))) x_(i, q)$.

  *(iii)* Expanding $(mu_p^((r)) - x_(i, q))^2 = (mu_p^((r)) - mu_p^((r + 1)) + mu_p^((r + 1)) - x_(i, q))^2$ and noting that the cross-term vanishes because $sum_((i, q) in cal(S)_p^((r))) (mu_p^((r + 1)) - x_(i, q)) = 0$ gives the parallel-axis identity
  $ Q(mu^((r)) | mu^((r))) - Q(mu^((r + 1)) | mu^((r))) = sum_(p = 1)^m d_p^((r)) (mu_p^((r)) - mu_p^((r + 1)))^2. $
  Combining $F_k (mu^((r))) = Q(mu^((r)) | mu^((r)))$, $F_k (mu^((r + 1))) <= Q(mu^((r + 1)) | mu^((r)))$, and $d_p^((r)) >= |C_k|$ yields the stated descent bound.

  *(iv)* Whenever $mu^((r + 1)) != mu^((r))$, $F_k$ strictly decreases by at least $|C_k| norm(mu^((r)) - mu^((r + 1)))_2^2 > 0$. Since each alignment tuple $(A_i)_(i in C_k)$ in the finite Cartesian product $product_(i in C_k) cal(A)_(m, L_i)$ determines a unique minimizer $mu$, no alignment tuple can be revisited once $F_k$ drops strictly. Thus inner DBA terminates in finitely many steps. Outer assignment steps also strictly decrease $J_"DBA"$ upon any label change, and because there are at most $K^n$ partitions, outer DTW $k$-Means terminates finitely.
]

#subsec("B", "Non-Convexity and Constructive Suboptimal DBA Fixed Point")

Despite Theorem 1's strict MM descent, $F_k$ is non-convex and DBA can become trapped at severely suboptimal local minima even for two length-3 sequences.

#theorem(title: "Non-Convexity of DTW Inertia and Strictly Suboptimal DBA Fixed Point", origin: "Original Counterexample")[
  Consider $x_1 = (0, 10, 12)^top$ and $x_2 = (0, 2, 12)^top$ in $bb(R)^3$. Then:
  (i) The DTW Fréchet objective $F(mu) = "DTW"^2 (mu, x_1) + "DTW"^2 (mu, x_2)$ is strictly non-convex on $bb(R)^3$;
  (ii) Initialized at the Euclidean mean $mu^((0)) = (x_1 + x_2) / 2 = (0, 6, 12)^top$, DBA has strictly unique optimal identity paths for both series and terminates in one step at $mu^((0))$ with $F(mu^((0))) = 32$, whereas $min_(mu in bb(R)^3) F(mu) <= 8 = F(mu^((0))) / 4$.
]

#proof[
  First bound $F(x_2)$ at $mu^ast = x_2 = (0, 2, 12)^top$. Trivially $"DTW"^2 (mu^ast, x_2) = 0$. Aligning $mu^ast$ to $x_1 = (0, 10, 12)^top$ along the admissible path $((1, 1), (2, 1), (3, 2), (3, 3)) in cal(A)_(3, 3)$ yields
  $ "DTW"^2 (mu^ast, x_1) <= (0 - 0)^2 + (2 - 0)^2 + (12 - 10)^2 + (12 - 12)^2 = 8. $
  Hence $F(x_2) <= 8$. Symmetrically, aligning $x_1$ to $x_2$ via $((1, 1), (1, 2), (2, 3), (3, 3))$ gives $F(x_1) <= 8$.

  Now evaluate the exact $3 times 3$ Bellman dynamic programming tables at $mu^((0)) = (0, 6, 12)^top$. Against $x_1 = (0, 10, 12)^top$, the local squared-error matrix $Delta(mu^((0)), x_1)$ and cumulative DP matrix $D^((1))$ are
  $ Delta^((1)) = mat(0, 100, 144; 36, 16, 36; 144, 4, 0), quad D^((1)) = mat(0, 100, 244; 36, 16, 52; 180, 20, 16). $
  Backtracking from $(3, 3)$: $min{D^((1))_(2, 2), D^((1))_(2, 3), D^((1))_(3, 2)} = min{16, 52, 20} = 16$ uniquely at $(2, 2)$, and $min{D^((1))_(1, 1), D^((1))_(1, 2), D^((1))_(2, 1)} = min{0, 100, 36} = 0$ uniquely at $(1, 1)$. Thus $A_1^((0)) = I_3$ is the *strictly unique* optimal path with $"DTW"^2 (mu^((0)), x_1) = 16$.

  Against $x_2 = (0, 2, 12)^top$, the local cost and DP matrices are
  $ Delta^((2)) = mat(0, 4, 144; 36, 16, 36; 144, 100, 0), quad D^((2)) = mat(0, 4, 148; 36, 16, 40; 180, 116, 16). $
  Backtracking again selects $(2, 2)$ uniquely ($16 < min{40, 116}$) and $(1, 1)$ uniquely ($0 < min{4, 36}$), so $A_2^((0)) = I_3$ is strictly unique with $"DTW"^2 (mu^((0)), x_2) = 16$.

  Consequently $F(mu^((0))) = 16 + 16 = 32$. Because $mu^((0)) = (x_1 + x_2) / 2$ satisfies
  $ F((x_1 + x_2) / 2) = 32 > 8 >= 1 / 2 F(x_1) + 1 / 2 F(x_2), $
  $F$ is strictly non-convex, proving (i). Furthermore, with $A_1^((0)) = A_2^((0)) = I_3$, the DBA update outputs $mu^((1)) = (x_1 + x_2) / 2 = mu^((0))$, proving that $mu^((0))$ is a strict local minimum and stationary fixed point of DBA with at least a four-fold optimality gap ($32$ vs. $<= 8$).
]

#subsec("C", "Affine Equivariance, Range Confinement, and Breakdown")

#proposition(title: "Equivariance, Convex-Hull Bounds, and Zero Breakdown", origin: "Original Proof")[
  Let ${mu^((r))}_(r >= 0)$ be the sequence of DBA iterates on cluster $C_k$. Then:
  (i) *Affine equivariance*: For any $a in bb(R) without {0}$ and $b in bb(R)$, mapping $x_i |-> a x_i + b bold(1)_(L_i)$ and $mu^((0)) |-> a mu^((0)) + b bold(1)_m$ preserves every optimal warping path and transforms $mu^((r)) |-> a mu^((r)) + b bold(1)_m$ for all $r >= 1$.
  (ii) *Range confinement*: Every $mu^((r))$ ($r >= 1$) lies in $[min_(i, q) x_(i, q), max_(i, q) x_(i, q)]^m$.
  (iii) *Breakdown point*: The finite-sample breakdown point of DBA is $epsilon^ast = 1 / |C_k|$.
]

#proof[
  *(i)* For every $(p, q)$, $((a mu_p + b) - (a x_(i, q) + b))^2 = a^2 (mu_p - x_(i, q))^2$. Because $a^2 > 0$, multiplying $Delta(mu, x_i)$ by $a^2$ leaves $arg min_(A in cal(A)_(m, L_i)) chevron.l A, Delta chevron.r$ unchanged. By induction on $r$,
  $ (d_p^((r)))^(-1) sum_((i, q) in cal(S)_p^((r))) (a x_(i, q) + b) = a mu_p^((r + 1)) + b. $

  *(ii)* Each coordinate $mu_p^((r + 1))$ is an unweighted average of entries from ${x_(i, q)}$ with positive denominator $d_p^((r)) >= |C_k|$, hence lies in the closed interval $[min_(i, q) x_(i, q), max_(i, q) x_(i, q)]$.

  *(iii)* Fix $n_k - 1$ series bounded by $|x_(i, q)| <= M$ and let a single corrupted series $x_1 = c bold(1)_(L_1)$ satisfy $c -> +infinity$. By the boundary condition of $cal(A)_(m, L_1)$, the pair $(1, 1)$ belongs to *every* admissible warping path $A_1^((0))$, so $(1, 1) in cal(S)_1^((0))$ regardless of initialization $mu^((0))$. Meanwhile, each series $x_i$ contributes at most $L_i$ pairs to row $1$, so $d_1^((0)) <= sum_(i in C_k) L_i =: L_"tot"$. Therefore,
  $ mu_1^((1)) = 1 / (d_1^((0))) sum_((i, q) in cal(S)_1^((0))) x_(i, q) >= (c - (L_"tot" - 1) M) / L_"tot" -> +infinity $
  as $c -> +infinity$. One corrupted series suffices to drive $norm(mu^((1)))_infinity -> infinity$, establishing $epsilon^ast = 1 / |C_k|$.
]

#subsec("D", "Delannoy Path Growth and Triangle Inequality Failure")

#lemma(title: "Exact Path Cardinality and Non-Metricity of DTW", origin: "Original Derivation")[
  (i) The cardinality of $cal(A)_(m, L)$ equals the Delannoy number @sakoe1978dtw
  $ |cal(A)_(m, L)| & = D(m - 1, L - 1) \
    & = sum_(j = 0)^(min(m - 1, L - 1)) binom(m - 1, j) binom(L - 1, j) 2^j, $
  which for $m = L$ satisfies $D(m - 1, m - 1) <= (3 + 2 sqrt(2))^(m - 1)$ and grows exponentially as $Theta((3 + 2 sqrt(2))^m / sqrt(m))$.
  (ii) Neither $"DTW"$ nor $"DTW"^2$ satisfies the triangle inequality on $bb(R)^4$.
]

#proof[
  *(i)* Every path in $cal(A)_(m, L)$ moves from $(1, 1)$ to $(m, L)$ via steps in ${(1, 0), (0, 1), (1, 1)}$, giving the Delannoy recurrence $D(u, v) = D(u - 1, v) + D(u, v - 1) + D(u - 1, v - 1)$ with $D(u, 0) = D(0, v) = 1$. Counting paths with $k$ diagonal $(1, 1)$ steps and rewriting via Vandermonde's identity yields the binomial sum $D(u, v) = sum_(j = 0)^(min(u, v)) binom(u, j) binom(v, j) 2^j$. For $u = v = m - 1$, applying $sum_j a_j^2 <= (sum_j a_j)^2$ to $a_j = binom(m - 1, j) 2^(j / 2) >= 0$ proves
  $ D(m - 1, m - 1) & <= (sum_(j = 0)^(m - 1) binom(m - 1, j) (sqrt(2))^j)^2 \
    & = (1 + sqrt(2))^(2(m - 1)) = (3 + 2 sqrt(2))^(m - 1). $

  *(ii)* Set $x = (0, 2, 2, 0)^top$, $z = (0, 0, 2, 0)^top$, and $y = (0, 0, 1, 0)^top$ in $bb(R)^4$. First, aligning $x$ to $z$ along $((1, 1), (1, 2), (2, 3), (3, 3), (4, 4))$ incurs zero cost at every pair, so $"DTW"(x, z) = 0$. Second, aligning $z$ to $y$ via the identity path gives $"DTW"^2 (z, y) <= (2 - 1)^2 = 1$; since every entry of row $3$ of $Delta(z, y)$ is $(2 - y_q)^2 >= 1$ and every admissible path visits row $3$, $"DTW"^2 (z, y) = 1$, hence $"DTW"(z, y) = 1$. Third, in $Delta(x, y)$, *every* entry of row $2$ and *every* entry of row $3$ satisfies $(2 - y_q)^2 >= 1$ (since $y_q in {0, 1}$). Because any admissible path visits both row $2$ and row $3$ at least once, $"DTW"^2 (x, y) >= 1 + 1 = 2$, attained by $((1, 1), (1, 2), (2, 3), (3, 3), (4, 4))$. Thus $"DTW"(x, y) = sqrt(2)$, giving the strict violations
  $ "DTW"(x, y) = sqrt(2) > 1 = "DTW"(x, z) + "DTW"(z, y), $
  and $"DTW"^2 (x, y) = 2 > 1 = "DTW"^2 (x, z) + "DTW"^2 (z, y)$.
]

#subsec("E", "Sakoe-Chiba Band Constraints and Complexity Bounds")

#proposition(title: "Band-Constrained DBA Descent and Exact Sandwich Bound", origin: "Classical — Cited")[
  For uniform length $m$ and Sakoe-Chiba band half-width $w in {0, dots, m - 1}$ @sakoe1978dtw, let $cal(A)_(m, m)^((w)) = {A in cal(A)_(m, m) : A_(p, q) = 1 => |p - q| <= w}$ and $"DTW"_w^2 (mu, x) = min_(A in cal(A)_(m, m)^((w))) chevron.l A, Delta(mu, x) chevron.r$. Then:
  (i) $"DTW"^2 (mu, x) <= "DTW"_w^2 (mu, x) <= norm(mu - x)_2^2$ for all $mu, x in bb(R)^m$;
  (ii) Constrained DBA retains the exact MM descent and finite termination of Theorem 1 @petitjean2011dba;
  (iii) Per-iteration DTW DP cost reduces from $m^2$ cells to $m(2 w + 1) - w(w + 1) = O(m w)$ cells.
]

#proof[
  *(i)* Immediate from the nested inclusions ${I_m} = cal(A)_(m, m)^((0)) subset.eq cal(A)_(m, m)^((w)) subset.eq cal(A)_(m, m)$, noting $chevron.l I_m, Delta(mu, x) chevron.r = norm(mu - x)_2^2$. *(ii)* Because $I_m in cal(A)_(m, m)^((w))$ and every $A in cal(A)_(m, m)^((w))$ is an admissible warping path visiting every row $p in {1, dots, m\}$ at least once, $d_p^((r)) >= |C_k|$ and finiteness of $cal(A)_(m, m)^((w))$ still hold verbatim, so the proof of Theorem 1 applies unchanged. *(iii)* Subtracting the two excluded triangular corners of size $sum_(t = 1)^(m - 1 - w) t$ from $m^2$ yields $m^2 - (m - w - 1)(m - w) = m(2 w + 1) - w(w + 1)$ active DP cells.
]

#sec("III", "Complexity, Geometry and Operational Profile")

In each outer Lloyd iteration, assignment evaluates $K n$ pairwise DTW distances, taking $O(K n m^2)$ time (or $O(K n m w)$ under a Sakoe-Chiba band of width $w$). During the update phase, each cluster $C_k$ executes at most $I$ inner DBA iterations, each aligning $|C_k|$ series against $mu_k$ in $O(|C_k| m^2)$ time plus $O(|C_k| m)$ accumulation for coordinate averages; summing across all $K$ clusters gives $O(I n m^2)$ per outer step and total time complexity $O(T (K + I) n m^2)$, or $O(T (K + I) n m w)$ when band-constrained.

Working memory is $O(n m + K m + m^2)$ because only one $m times m$ DP table (and backpointer table) needs to reside in cache at a time; for assignment-only evaluation without path traceback, rolling two rows lowers DP scratch space to $O(m)$, whereas DBA's traceback requires $O(m^2)$ (or $O(m w)$ banded) integer backpointers. Unlike Euclidean $k$-Means, DBA's Voronoi cells in $bb(R)^m$ are non-convex unions of piecewise-quadratic regions bounded by equality hypersurfaces between competing warping paths, and because DTW violates the triangle inequality (Lemma 1), metric pruning trees such as Elkan's algorithm cannot be applied directly without specialized lower bounds (e.g., LB\_Keogh).

