#sec("V", "Structural Relations and Empirical Behavior")

DBA generalizes Lloyd's $k$-Means continuously through the warping-band radius $w$ and forms the zero-temperature boundary of Soft-DTW Clustering (Alg. 164).

#proposition(title: "Zero-Band Reduction to Lloyd's k-Means", origin: "Original Derivation")[
  On equal-length series $X subset bb(R)^m$, setting the Sakoe-Chiba band half-width to $w = 0$ collapses $cal(A)_(m, m)^((0)) = {I_m}$, reduces $"DTW"_0^2 (mu, x)$ identically to $norm(mu - x)_2^2$, sets every coordinate valence to $d_p^((r)) = |C_k|$, and causes inner DBA to converge in exactly $I = 1$ step to the Euclidean cluster mean $mu_k = |C_k|^(-1) sum_(i in C_k) x_i$ of Lloyd's $k$-Means @lloyd1982least.
]

#proof[
  At $w = 0$, the constraint $|p - q| <= 0$ forces $A_(p, q) = 0$ for $p != q$, leaving $I_m$ as the sole admissible matrix in $cal(A)_(m, m)^((0))$. Hence $W_i = "diag"(I_m bold(1)_m) = I_m$, $d_p = |C_k|$, and $mu^((1)) = (|C_k| I_m)^(-1) sum_(i in C_k) I_m x_i = |C_k|^(-1) sum_(i in C_k) x_i$. Since $I_m$ does not depend on $mu$, $mu^((2)) = mu^((1))$, terminating in one inner step identically to Lloyd's $k$-Means update.
]

In the opposite direction, replacing the hard minimum in $F_k (mu)$ with the entropic soft-minimum $min^gamma$ yields Soft-DTW Clustering (Alg. 164) @cuturi2017softdtw, which recovers DBA's hard objective uniformly as $gamma -> 0^+$. Compared with k-Shape (Alg. 162), DBA replaces rigid shift-invariant cross-correlation with local elastic warping and retains raw amplitude scales rather than imposing per-series $z$-normalization.

On the ClusterOverview four-dataset benchmark, DBA exhibits strong overall cluster separation measured by Silhouette @rousseeuw1987silhouettes and ARI @hubert1985comparing: Iris $"Sil" = 0.449$ ($"ARI" = 0.608$), Customers $"Sil" = 0.328$, Multi-Shape $"Sil" = 0.459$ ($"ARI" = "N/A"$), and High-D $"Sil" = 0.249$ ($"ARI" = "N/A"$), with a mean runtime of $26.159$ ms. Preserving coordinate amplitudes gives DBA a decisive lead over k-Shape across every dataset (e.g., Iris $"ARI"$ rises from $0.397$ to $0.608$, Customers $"Sil"$ from $0.181$ to $0.328$, High-D $"Sil"$ from $0.055$ to $0.249$), while closed-form coordinate averaging keeps runtime $5.6 times$ faster than Soft-DTW ($147.236$ ms) at the expense of slightly lower Multi-Shape Silhouette ($0.459$ vs. $0.603$) and Iris ARI ($0.608$ vs. $0.661$) where smooth alignment averaging helps escape sharp local minima of the kind proven in Theorem 2.
